WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.198
Vert.x runs on Netty.

2
00:00:04.198 --> 00:00:12.399
A Vert.x instance starts a group of Netty event loops, by default twice the number of cores: 8 on a 4-core machine.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.685
What Vert.x adds is a rule on top of those loops: the context.

4
00:00:16.685 --> 00:00:22.021
A context is the home of a piece of code, and it decides the thread that runs it.

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:27.947
Each verticle you deploy gets its own context, tied to one event loop for life.

6
00:00:27.947 --> 00:00:34.023
Every handler it registers, for HTTP, timers or the event bus, runs on that context.

7
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:39.269
So a verticle's code runs on one thread, one task at a time.

8
00:00:39.269 --> 00:00:42.721
Three events for verticle A arrive at the same moment.

9
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:46.429
The loop runs them one after another, never at once.

10
00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:48.984
So A's field, approved, needs no lock.

11
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.010
The catch: that thread is shared.

12
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.891
Verticle B's context sits on the same loop.

13
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.914
Now A's handler makes a 500 millisecond JDBC call, right on the loop.

14
00:00:59.914 --> 00:01:06.612
The loop can do nothing else until it returns, so B's requests wait, though B did nothing wrong.

15
00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:11.304
executeBlocking moves the blocking part to a worker thread.

16
00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:14.977
A's handler returns at once, and the loop is free for B.

17
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.369
When the query is done, the Future completes back on A's context, on loop 0.

18
00:01:20.369 --> 00:01:25.197
So the reply runs on A's own thread, and A's state still needs no lock.

19
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.806
One catch: executeBlocking is ordered by default, so calls from one context still run one at a time.

20
00:01:32.806 --> 00:01:35.287
Pass false when they are independent.

21
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:43.189
In the lesson's demo, four 500 millisecond queries took 2 seconds ordered, and half a second with false.

22
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.868
Vert.x calls this the golden rule: don't block the event loop.

23
00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:54.168
A checker thread looks every second, and warns when one task has held a loop for more than 2 seconds.
